Not quite.

You said "hers" is a possessive pronoun (which is correct and which I 
affirmed), and possessive pronouns can be used as nouns or adjectives 
(which is incorrect and which I attempted to correct).

Consider the sentence "That is his horse." to "That horse is his." 
"His" can be used as two different parts of speech (possessive pronoun 
or possessive adjective), and so can be used in either sentence.

Next compare the sentence "That is her horse." to "That horse is hers." 
  "Hers" is a possessive pronoun *only* and cannot be used as an 
possessive adjective ("That is hers horse."), while "her" is an 
possessive adjective and cannot be used as a possessive pronoun ("That 
horse is her.").  (Of course, "her" is also a objective pronoun 
corresponding to "him", so that last sentence could mean that she had 
bad luck with a polymorph spell.)


